INT. DEFUNCT CLOWN COLLEGE, DORMITORY HALL - MORNING

Peeling circus-stripe wallpaper. Trunks in the corner. Three oversized shoes on a shoe rack, one of them nesting a pigeon.

PROFESSOR BARNABY QUILLFEATHER (60s, ringmaster's coat over pajamas, magnificent mustache) sits on a cot in his stocking feet, holding a custard pie in each hand like a man weighing his sins. Across from him, MINNIE FLOCKHART (30s, her red nose hanging on a string around her neck) stuffs a polka-dot suitcase. Beside her, DUKE "DOODLE" OKAFOR-LINN (40s, half-face of white greasepaint) retapes a unicycle tire.

BARNABY
Minnie, my dear, you can't leave.

MINNIE
I have a bus ticket, Barnaby.

BARNABY
A bus ticket is a coward's instrument.

DOODLE
It's a Greyhound.

BARNABY
Doodle. Please. Say something to her.

DOODLE
Minnie, don't leave.

MINNIE
Doodle, stop.

BARNABY
I forbid it.

MINNIE
^I'm thirty-four!

Silence. Barnaby looks into the pie in his right hand. Doodle rolls the unicycle forward and back.

BARNABY
Then I shall tell you a story, and you will stay, because the story is true.

MINNIE
Barnaby, it's always the same story.

BARNABY
It is never the same story. It is the same ending.

MINNIE
That's what I said.

BARNABY
Allow me.

He stands. The cot creaks. He sets both pies on the footlocker, extremely carefully.

BARNABY (CONT'D)
In nineteen seventy-one, a young man arrived at this very hall with a suitcase, a bad mustache, and a dream. He was told he had no talent. He was told this by a woman who is now deceased and whose pie he now owns. He was told that comedy is a discipline of falling. Do you know what he did?

MINNIE
He fell.

BARNABY
He fell. Every day. For fifty years. And every day he stood up again, because falling is the only part of this profession that is not an act.

DOODLE
(sniffling)
That's beautiful, Prof.

MINNIE
It's a lovely speech. It's also what you said when the roof leaked.

BARNABY
That, too, was a fall.

MINNIE
Barnaby. They closed the school.

BARNABY
Not in spirit.

MINNIE
In every other way.

BARNABY
(a pause)
Yes.

The door creaks. HARRIET "HATTIE" VON BLOOM (70s, severe gray bun, wheeling a suitcase of her own) stands in the doorway. She looks at the pies, the mustache, the unicycle, the suitcase.

HATTIE
I received your letter.

BARNABY
Hattie.

HATTIE
You said the school was dying. You said you needed a clown who could weep and a clown who could fall.

MINNIE
That's Doodle and me.

HATTIE
I am the clown who can do both.

DOODLE
Prof, who is this?

BARNABY
(faint)
My former wife.

MINNIE
(to Doodle, low)
I've never heard of a former wife.

DOODLE
(low)
Me neither.

HATTIE
Barnaby. Put down your pie.

BARNABY
It's on the footlocker.

HATTIE
Then pick it up and throw it.

Barnaby looks at her. A long beat. Then, with the care of a surgeon, he lifts one pie and hands it to her, face-first.

HATTIE (CONT'D)
(smiling for the first time)
Thank you.

She sets it on his head. Minnie closes the suitcase. She sits. Doodle starts to laugh.
